WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.339
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.339 --> 00:00:09.520
This paper is about the absolute favorite
thing of computer graphics researchers: destroying

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.900
virtual objects in the most creative ways.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.780
This is the only place on Earth where words
like deformable bodies and cutting can be

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.330
used in the same sentence and be delighted
about it.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:26.170
This time around, we are going to cut and
dismember every virtual object that stands

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:27.170
in our way.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:28.500
And then some more.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.070
In these animations, we have complex 3D geometry,
and the objective is to change these geometries

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:39.429
in a way that remains physically correct even
in the presence of complex cut surfaces.

00:00:39.429 --> 00:00:44.120
When such a cut happens, traditional techniques
typically delete and duplicate parts of the

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.260
geometry close to the cut.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.550
This is a heavily simplified solution that
leads to inaccurate results.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:55.549
Other techniques try to rebuild parts of the
geometry that are affected by the cut.

00:00:55.549 --> 00:00:59.949
This is what computer graphics researchers
like to call remeshing, and it works quite

00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:02.109
well, but it takes ages to perform.

00:01:02.109 --> 00:01:07.390
Also, is still has drawbacks, for instance,
quantities like temperature and deformations

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.830
also have to be transferred to the new geometry,
which is non-trivial to execute properly.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:17.979
In this work, a new technique is proposed
that is able to process really complex cuts

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:20.000
without creating new geometry.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.759
No remeshing takes place, but the mass and
stiffness properties of the materials are

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:25.979
retained correctly.

00:01:25.979 --> 00:01:31.329
Also, the fact that it minimizes the geometric
processing overhead leads to a not only simpler,

00:01:31.329 --> 00:01:33.219
but a more efficient solution.

00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:38.859
There is so much visual detail in the results
that I could watch this video ten times and

00:01:38.859 --> 00:01:40.391
still find something new in there.

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:47.799
There are also some horrifying, Game of Thrones
kinda experiments in this footage.

00:01:47.799 --> 00:01:49.549
Watch out!

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:51.170
Ouch!

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:55.409
The presentation of the results and the part
of the video that compares against a previous

00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:57.560
technique is absolutely brilliant.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:58.740
You have to see it.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.850
The paper is also remarkably well written,
make sure to have a look at that too.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.909
The link is available in the video description.

00:02:04.909 --> 00:02:10.200
I am really itching to make some longer videos
where we can go into some of these derivations

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.070
and build a strong intuitive understanding
of them.

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:17.551
That sounds like a ton of fun, and if this
could ever become a full time endeavor, I

00:02:17.551 --> 00:02:22.250
am more than enthused to start doing more
and work on bonus videos like that.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:25.920
If you enjoyed this episode, don't forget
to hit the like button and subscribe to the

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:26.920
series.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:31.170
Normally, it's up to YouTube to decide whether
you get a notification or not, so make sure

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:35.280
to click the bell icon as well to never miss
a Two Minute Papers episode.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.520
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

